    "content": [
      "At Blend Conference 2024, Albert Shum challenged designers to think critically about their role in shaping artificial intelligence. His talk centered on a crucial premise: innovation requires spotting patterns and bringing people together, and inclusive design demands examining different lenses of exclusion.",
      "Shum posed critical questions that every AI designer must grapple with: What is fairness? Who's being left behind? Who's responsible? He argued forcefully that techno-optimism won't solve ethical challenges—in fact, it represents another form of colonization that designers must actively resist.",
      "The talk called for an evolution of design frameworks. The traditional triad of Feasible, Desirable, and Viable is no longer sufficient. Shum proposed adding \"Responsible\" as a fourth pillar, acknowledging that this dimension needs to be explicitly included in our design thinking.",
      "Understanding AGI (Artificial General Intelligence) requires grasping its design context: the source of training data matters deeply, and probabilistic (not deterministic) systems create variance and emergence that designers must account for. As the transformer architecture paper famously stated, \"Attention is all you need\"—but designers must be attentive to the right things.",
      "Navigating change with purpose became a central theme. Shum referenced the Kübler-Ross change curve and hype cycles to illustrate how organizations and individuals process technological shifts. True leadership means seeking professional connections outside of work and setting boundaries about how we perform at work versus our worth as human beings.",
      "When designing with AI, Shum emphasized that human contributions remain essential: empathy, synthesis, and an evolved design process. Designers must learn to \"design the language\"—crafting the prompts and interactions that shape how humans and AI collaborate. This isn't about replacing designers; it's about expanding what design can be."
    ]
